WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.966
Welcome to Horizon
installer training, Part 5.

2
00:00:05.544 --> 00:00:07.082
Right way, wrong way.

3
00:00:07.532 --> 00:00:10.652
By the end, you will be
able to open a box and tell,

4
00:00:10.652 --> 00:00:14.203
fast, whether it is wired
the old way or the right way.

5
00:00:14.653 --> 00:00:17.794
And you will know what a tripping
breaker is really telling you.

6
00:00:19.144 --> 00:00:21.951
These two boxes look almost
the same when you open them.

7
00:00:21.951 --> 00:00:23.654
They are not the same at all.

8
00:00:24.104 --> 00:00:26.781
Here is the wrong way.
This is what we are fixing.

9
00:00:27.231 --> 00:00:31.951
One 14/2 wire feeds all three
injections. Nothing keeps them apart.

10
00:00:32.401 --> 00:00:37.097
There are no breakers. The 600
watt supply has 25 amps available.

11
00:00:37.547 --> 00:00:40.270
That is 180 lights behind one path.

12
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.871
If one shorts, nothing shuts off.
The wire just keeps getting hotter.

13
00:00:46.221 --> 00:00:48.677
Now the right way. This
is the standard now.

14
00:00:49.127 --> 00:00:52.732
Own wire, own breaker.
Each group stands alone.

15
00:00:53.182 --> 00:00:56.416
The board has 6 breakers, 4 amps max each.

16
00:00:56.416 --> 00:00:59.899
Each group here is 60 lights, at 3.4 amps.

17
00:01:00.349 --> 00:01:04.698
If one group shorts, that one breaker
trips. The other two stay lit.

18
00:01:05.148 --> 00:01:07.035
And you know exactly where to look.

19
00:01:07.485 --> 00:01:09.153
Compare that to the wrong way,

20
00:01:09.153 --> 00:01:13.574
where one short leaves 180 lights on
a wire that just keeps getting hotter.

21
00:01:14.924 --> 00:01:18.030
So when you open a box,
ask three questions.

22
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.343
One. Is there a breaker for
every wire leaving the box?

23
00:01:21.793 --> 00:01:24.839
If not, it is an old system.
A Mark 1 or a Mark 2.

24
00:01:24.839 --> 00:01:27.952
You will learn what to
do with those in Part 7.

25
00:01:28.147 --> 00:01:31.514
Two. Does each wire go to one
injection only? Follow it.

26
00:01:31.514 --> 00:01:33.447
If it branches, it is wrong.

27
00:01:33.897 --> 00:01:39.662
One wire branching off to two or more injections
is exactly the wrong box you just saw.

28
00:01:40.112 --> 00:01:43.705
Three. Is each breaker 4
amps? Bigger than 4 is wrong.

29
00:01:44.155 --> 00:01:46.658
And do not fit a bigger
one to stop a trip.

30
00:01:48.008 --> 00:01:51.648
Which brings us to this. A
tripping breaker is good news.

31
00:01:52.098 --> 00:01:54.101
It means the safety is working.

32
00:01:54.551 --> 00:01:57.529
It is telling you the circuit
has too many lights on it.

33
00:01:57.979 --> 00:02:01.132
So fix the circuit. Never
fit a bigger breaker.

34
00:02:01.582 --> 00:02:03.867
A bigger breaker does not fix anything.

35
00:02:03.867 --> 00:02:08.043
It just lets more amps into thin
wire, and that is how fires start.

36
00:02:08.493 --> 00:02:11.803
Remember the wrong-way box. It
had no breaker to trip at all,

37
00:02:11.803 --> 00:02:14.061
so the wire just kept getting hotter.

38
00:02:14.511 --> 00:02:18.127
Count the lights first. It is
almost always more than 70.

39
00:02:19.477 --> 00:02:24.128
Last, the card to keep in the truck. It
puts the whole standard in one place.

40
00:02:24.578 --> 00:02:31.863
The numbers. 60 lights per circuit, 70
at the most. 4.0 amps max. 96 watts max.

41
00:02:32.313 --> 00:02:37.544
At least 22.8 volts at the last
light. And 16/2 FT-4 for the feeder.

42
00:02:37.994 --> 00:02:40.451
The math. Lights are feet times 1.5.

43
00:02:40.451 --> 00:02:43.840
Circuits are lights
divided by 60, rounded up.

44
00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:48.936
Watts are lights times 1.37.
Amps are watts divided by 24.

45
00:02:48.936 --> 00:02:51.750
Check that amps are 4.0 or less.

46
00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.752
And every time. Power off
before you plug anything in.

47
00:02:55.752 --> 00:02:57.976
Test on PW, never on a colour.

48
00:02:58.426 --> 00:03:00.423
Cut the positive at every injection.

49
00:03:00.423 --> 00:03:03.668
Grease every connector. And
meter it before you leave.

50
00:03:05.018 --> 00:03:06.313
Let's lock it in.

51
00:03:06.763 --> 00:03:09.778
Wrong way. One wire
feeding several injections,

52
00:03:09.778 --> 00:03:12.273
and no breakers. Nothing shuts off.

53
00:03:12.723 --> 00:03:18.058
Right way. Own wire, own 4 amp breaker.
One group trips, and the rest stay lit.

54
00:03:18.508 --> 00:03:20.948
Open the box, and ask three questions.

55
00:03:20.948 --> 00:03:25.178
A breaker for every wire? One
injection per wire? 4 amps each?

56
00:03:25.628 --> 00:03:30.360
A trip is the safety working. Fix the
circuit. Never fit a bigger breaker.

57
00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.660
Keep the truck card
with you, on every job.

58
00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:39.445
Next, take the Part 5 quiz. Then
in Part 6, we test before we leave.
